== The Sixth Sermon

#quote[And He will swallow up death in victory, and God will wipe away tears from all faces, so that the rebukes of His people may be taken away from the earth; for the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.—Isa 25:8.]

You#super[+] have heard before of a feast provided for God’s people, its founder being God himself, who alone can truly comfort the soul and the conscience (which is what most especially needs comfort), since he is above the conscience. The place where the feast is kept is “Mount Zion,” the church of God. The delicacies are described as “fat things, wine refined on the lees,” the best of the best that can be thought of, which is Christ with all his benefits; he is bread indeed and drink indeed, who cherishes and nourishes the soul to everlasting life. And so that there should be nothing to disturb the solemnity of the feast, he promises to “destroy the face of the covering,” “to take away the veil spread over all nations,” the veil of ignorance and unbelief, so as to shine on the soul and fill it full of knowledge and heavenly comfort. And because there can be no comfort where death is feared, death being the greatest enemy in this life, he will therefore “swallow up death in victory,” and all that makes way for death or attends it. And when death is taken away, all its attendants vanish with it: “God will wipe away all tears from all faces.” Because the best things do not have the best welcome in the world, nor the best persons either, God promises that the rebukes of his people will be taken away from off the earth; what they are, they will be known to be. These are very great matters, and therefore there is a great confirmation; they have a seal. And what is that? “The #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.”

Last time I showed that God’s children will shed tears, and that they have cause to do so. I will now enlarge on it a little.

It is the condition of men since the fall. In paradise, before, there was no cause of tears; nothing was out of joint, all was in order. There was no sin, therefore no sorrow, therefore no sense of sorrow. And so in heaven there will be no tears, because there is no cause for them; they will be as far from heaven as their cause. This life is a valley of tears, a life of misery, and therefore we shed tears here. And we lack no cause for them as long as there is sin in the world, and sorrow, and the misery that follows sin: our own sins and the sins of others, our own miseries and the miseries of others. And surely a child of God finds this the greatest cause of mourning in this world, that he has a principle in him always troubling him in the service of God. He cannot serve God with the cheerfulness he would. His unfeelingness, that he cannot be so aware of God dishonored by himself and others, is his burden. He is grieved that he cannot grieve enough. He can find tears for other things, matter enough for them, as the heathen man could say. A man loses his goods and has tears for them, but has to force tears for other things which are the true ground of grief. A child of God has a remainder of corruption, which pushes him on to offend against God and hinders him in his service, in the freedom and cheerfulness of it. And this he complains of with Paul and others: “What a wretched man I am!” not for his affliction, though that was great, but “Who will rescue me from this body of death?” (Rom 7:24).

Case 1. I will add a case here. Some say they cannot weep, but they can grieve; is it necessary, then, to weep or not? Tears are taken for the spring of tears; grief, all grief, will be taken away. Tears are only the messengers of grief; and often the deepest understanding, which takes things deeply, cannot express itself in tears. In some, the passages that carry the thought to the heart are more tender, so that they can weep. Now, the grief of a Christian is a grief of the judgment, a rational grief. Not only must felt tears have a felt grief, but a Christian’s grief is a felt grief and a grief of the judgment. He has a right judgment of the things that cause sorrow, and wills it; and tears are only an expression of it.

But how will I know whether grief is right or not? There are tears God has no bottle for. “You put my tears in Your bottle” (Ps 56:8). He makes much of them. They are vinum angelicum [the wine of angels], as he says. God is an angel to his people, to wipe away their tears. But there are some tears God has no bottle for: hypocritical tears, Delilah’s tears, tears of revenge and anger, Esau’s tears. And therefore the true tears that God will wipe away are, first of all, such as follow our condition here, our misery. God will wipe them away. If we speak of tears from a ground in the judgment,

+ The spring of true tears is the love of God, and of Christ, and of his church, and the love of the state of Christianity. Tears spring from love, these tears especially.

  Oh! a Christian takes it to heart that God should be so badly used in the world; that Christ, the Savior of the world, should meet with such treatment; that he himself should have anything in him that should offend such a Savior! This unkindness stings him to the heart. He takes it grievously that God should be abused. Laetitia habet suas lachrymas: there is not only grief, which is the immediate cause of tears, but another cause before it, that is, love. Joy likewise has its tears, though they are not especially meant here.

+ Again, tears are good and sound when we weep for our own sins as well as for the sins and miseries of others. And I will add more: we must weep for the sins of others as well as for our own. For it is a greater sign of the truth of grace to take the sins of others to heart more than our own. You#super[+] will say this is a kind of paradox. But often a man may take his own sins to heart as matter of terror of conscience, not as sins, as contrary to God, having an antipathy to him, being opposite to the state of the soul, not as sin is properly sin, but he is grieved and vexed for sin as it brings vexation and terror of conscience. When a man can take the sins of another to heart, and that truly, as an offense against his good God and a crucifying again of his sweet Savior, these are true tears indeed. It is more a sign of grace than to weep for a man’s own sins.

  Some are so taken up with terrors of conscience that they let their children, family, and friends alone. Their heart is eaten up with self-love, and they are nearly eaten up with their own terrors of conscience. But here is true grief and a hatred of sin in the right respect, when it exercises itself on others as well as on ourselves.

+ Again, tears arise from the right spring, from true grief, when we can weep in secret. Oh! says Jeremiah, if you#super[+] do so and so, “My soul will weep in secret because of your pride” (Jer 13:17). Here was a good soul indeed. Many will have tears of comfort in public. Yes, but when they can weep in secret for their own sins and the sins of others, it is evidence of a right spring of grief.
+ Again, when tears tend to the reformation of what they grieve for; otherwise they are steriles lachrymae, barren tears. Do they tend to reform what we weep for? Do they tend to action? Affections are good when they carry us to action; grief, love, joy, they are all for action. When we weep and grieve, and reform as well, it is a good sign. I will name no more. You#super[+] see, then, that grief is sound when it springs from the love of God, and is for the sins of others as well as our own, and for our own as well as others’; when it stirs us up to reformation; when it is in secret. And therefore let us examine our grief by these and similar evidences. It will be a good mark of a gracious soul. Then God will act as a sweet nurse, or a loving mother to her child that sheds tears. God will “wipe away all these tears.” Oh, the surpassing love of God! His love is a tender love: the love of a mother, the love of a nurse! It is not love only, but the very tenderness of love, tender mercy and compassion. How low he stoops to wipe away the tears of his children! “God will wipe away all tears.”

I will put one question more, and then go on. But we are told to rejoice always. Why then is it required that we weep and mourn? Can two contraries stand together?

Case 2. I answer: very well. For we may grieve, as we have matter of grief and are in a condition of grief; and we may rejoice, and ought to rejoice, as we look to the promise that God “will wipe away all tears.” When we think of the present cause, we cannot help but grieve; but when we look beyond all troubles, we cannot help but rejoice; it has an influence of joy on our heart. Indeed, for the present we may at times rejoice and grieve without looking to eternity. If we consider that we have offended God, done what grieves his Spirit, that is matter of grief. But when we consider that we have Christ at God’s right hand, who speaks peace for us and makes our peace by virtue of his mediation, that gives comfort. So we have cause of joy and cause of grief about the same things at the same time.

We are never in such a state of grief here but there is cause of joy, if we look about us, look forward, look upward. A Christian, that is, a good Christian, is a person who has many things to look after, so that he may manage his state of Christianity wisely. He is to look to himself and his sins, to the mercies of God in Christ and their constancy, which answers to their fruit in peace and joy here and happiness hereafter, which are constant too. God’s grace, like God himself, is constant, its fruits constant. Therefore “rejoice always.” And the apostle says, “I know what I say, I say it advisedly: rejoice always” (Phil 4:4). So the life of a Christian is a mixed life; indeed, the ground of our joy is our sorrow and grief, and joy is sown in grief. If we would rejoice indeed, let us mourn indeed. True joy arises and springs out of sorrow.

I go on to the next point. “And the rebukes of His people will be taken away from off the face of the earth.” Another benefit that makes the feast sweet and comforting is this: “He will take away the rebukes of His people.” And here the same method is to be used: that God’s children, his church and people, are under rebukes and under reproach. We need not stop to prove the truth of it. It is true, first, that the head of the church, and the church itself, and every particular member go under rebukes. As for the head of the church, we would spend the time to no purpose proving it. What was Christ’s life? It was under a veil. He did not appear to be what he was. You#super[+] know he was thought the chief of devils, an enemy to his prince, to Caesar. I will not spend time on clear truths.

As for the church itself, you#super[+] see in the book of Esther 3:8, “There is a strange people that acknowledge no law; they are against the laws of the prince.” They pass under the charge of being rebels. The poor church, which had thoughts of peace, the meek church of God, they counted enemies of the state, as Christ, the head, was. And so the church in Babylon, under what rebukes was it? They reproached them: “By the waters of Babylon we sat down and wept, when they said, ‘Sing us one of the songs of Zion’” (Ps 137:1). The church sits by the waters of Babylon all this life. The world is a kind of Babylon to God’s people, and then: Sing us one of your#super[+] songs. Where is your#super[+] God now? say the hearts of wretched people, when they saw the people of God in disgrace. Cicero could say of the nation of the Jews, “It shows how God regards it; it has been conquered so often.” So the heathen man could scorn the state of God’s people. You#super[+] see how the psalmist complains in the name of particular Christians: “Where is his God? He trusted in Him; let Him save him” (Ps 22:8). Oh, this was daggers to David’s heart. “It pierced my heart when they said, ‘Where is your God?’” (Ps 42:10). To touch a Christian in his God, as if God had no care for him, is more than his own grief and affliction. So it is when a child of God is rebuked and affronted, when religion must suffer by it. So the head of the church and the members of the church are under rebukes, as could be proved if I took you#super[+] through all the histories.

At this day you#super[+] see what the church of the Jews has come to, under what reproach the nation of the Jews is. And surely this prophecy aims partly at the conversion of the Jews. It will be fulfilled at the resurrection, when all tears will be perfectly wiped away. But it has a relation to the conversion of the Jews. In what state are they now? Are they not a byword of reproach? Moses’s saying is verified in them: “They will be a hissing to all nations” (Deut 28:37). And is it not a proverb, “Hated like a Jew”?

Reason. But what is the reason for it? Not to dwell long on the point, you#super[+] know there are two seeds in the world, the seed of the serpent and the seed of the woman; and the enmity between them is the true ground of it, and the antipathy in the hearts of carnal men to goodness. There is a light that shines in the life of those who are good, and those who are evil hate the light, as it reveals them to themselves and to the world not to be what they seem to be. There is a saltiness in the truth. It is savory, but it is tart, whether in the word preached or however else truth lays open what is contrary to corruption. And from this, pride and self-love in carnal men schemes how to overcast, as far as they can, the names of those who are better than themselves with a cloud of disgrace. It is the property of vile men to make all others vile, so that they may be alike. Men cannot bear distinctions of one from another. The Scripture distinguishes the “righteous man, more excellent than his neighbor” (Prov 12:26), but they will not have that. The hatred of distinction is the reason they make everyone as bad as they can. And this is why good things were never clothed in the right dress, nor evil things either, but pass under a veil. They take away the true garment of grace and holiness and goodness and put a false veil on it. It does not pass in this world for what it is, because wicked men will not allow it, but will raise up the credit of other things, of empty learning, or empty things, or vain courses, and cry up the credit of worldly things, so that those who are carried after those things may seem to be wise and not fools. The best things never had the happiness of passing under their own names, but had other coverings. Truth always goes with a torn and scratched face; it is a stranger in the world and meets with a stranger’s welcome.

Use 1. If this is so, we ought to beware of laying a scandal or reproach on religion. Salvian complains that in his time wickedness had gained such a head that those who were good and honorable, mali esse volunt, ne a malis abhorreantur: those who were good strove to be vile, so that they might not be vilified by others. “Oh,” he says, “how much is Christ indebted to the world, that those who acknowledge him, and acknowledge goodness, and acknowledge his cause, should be counted base for that very reason, because they are his friends!” Beware of taking offense.

Use 2. We need to be wise, so that we are not taken in this snare of Satan, to mistake error for truth and good for evil. Satan and his agents make things pass under contrary representations. Superstition passes for religion, and religion for superstition, schism, and heresy. It has always been so. Therefore seek wisdom to discern rightly. The devil has two properties: he is a liar and a murderer. The one makes way for the other, for he could not murder unless he lied. The devil himself will not be an open murderer if he can help it. Fraudulent persecution is worse than violent persecution. If he can bring men to hell by fraud and lying, he will never do it by violence. He is a liar so that he may be a murderer; for when he can raise a charge against the church and children of God, that they are rebels, enemies of the state, then he may be a murderer cum privilegio [with license]. When he has tainted God’s people in the opinion of the world, then they find the treatment not that they deserve, but that they are thought to deserve, when other men’s opinion of them is poisoned. “Oh, people everywhere are speaking against this sect,” they say to Paul (Acts 28:22). Therefore we need to be wise; for if the instruments of Satan, led by his spirit, had not hoped that slanders would take, they would never have been so skillful in that trade. But they know they will find some shallow fools who will believe them without searching into the depth of them, and will take up persons and things with prejudice. It is enough for these fools that this is said of them. They have neither wit nor judgment, nor so much patience, from following their lusts, as to examine them; and that is what makes them as mad as they are. Calumniare audacter, aliquid haerebit, slander boldly, something will stick; they are sure of it. What has raised and ruined many a man is Haman’s way of casting suspicion on those who are better than themselves. That was Haman’s trick, and so it will be the practice of the wicked, as it has been from the beginning, to the end of the world. “You are no friend of Caesar,” they say, and that is enough for Pilate (John 19:12). So it has been, and so it will be to the end of the world. Therefore we need to be wise, so that we are not misled. Men will never stop speaking evil till they have learned to speak better, till the Spirit of God has taught them.

Now, it is said that Christ will take away the rebukes of his people. That is the promise. As they are, they will be known to be. He will set all in joint again. Harmony is a sweet thing, and order is a sweet thing. The time will come when things that are now out of order in appearance will all be set in their due order again. Those that are basest will be lowest, and those that are excellent will be highest. This is at work and being framed now. In this confusion we must look to the catastrophe, the conclusion of all. He will “take away the rebukes of all.” God is the father of truth, and truth is the daughter of time. Time will bring forth truth at last. And those who are honorable indeed will be honorable. It is as true as God is just; for goodness and holiness are beams of God, and will he allow them always to pass under a false veil? There is not an attribute of God but will shine forth gloriously, even all his excellency and dignity. There is nothing that will be above him and his excellency. No, though for a while he seems not to rule in the world, or to have power, but allows those who are his enemies to get away with it, he is working another thing by allowing them: he is working the glory of his children and the confusion of his enemies. There is nothing in God but will gloriously shine, and nothing in his children, no beams of God, but will gloriously shine, to the confusion of the world. Those who are good will be known to be good; God will bring their righteousness to light. The witnesses who vexed the world and had base treatment were slain and disgraced, but they rose again and were carried up to heaven (Rev 11:12), like Elijah. So there will be a resurrection of name, a resurrection of reputation. What is good will be good, and what is bad will be bad. It will be known to be as it is. This is for comfort.

Use 1. You#super[+] hear, therefore, what course to take under disgrace. What shall we do when the church passes under disgrace, as it does now? A Protestant is worse than a Turk or a Jew among the railing papists. Among ourselves we see under what reputation the best things go. It is too well known to speak of. And the offense taken from this hardens men extremely. It keeps men from religion; it draws many from religion who have entered into it, because they have not learned so much self-denial as to risk disgrace. And surely where there is no self-denial there is no religion. Christ knew what doctrine he was teaching when he taught self-denial in this respect.

What shall we do, therefore? 1. Labor first of all for innocence, so that if men will reproach, they may reproach without cause.

#[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ Then labor for a spirit of patience to serve Christ with. “Great is your#super[+] reward when men speak evil of you” (Matt 5:12), for a good cause. It is the portion of a Christian in this life to do well and suffer ill. Of all men, certainly those are best who, out of love for goodness, are carried to goodness without looking to rewards or disgrace; who follow it with a single eye. Labor, therefore, for patience, and not only that, but,
+ For courage. For the moon goes its course and lets the dog bark. We have a course to run; let us keep our course constantly, and pass through good reports and bad reports; let us be settled, whatever the world thinks. We seek applause in another theater than the world.
+ Again, then, labor for sincerity under rebukes, that we have a good aim, such an aim as Paul had: “If I am mad and out of my wits” (2 Cor 5:13, 14). Because he was earnest for his Master, Christ, they counted him out of his wits. If I am out of my wits, it is for Christ. “If I am sober, it is for you#super[+]; the love of Christ compels me to be so” (2 Cor 5:14). Get the love of Christ, and that will make a man care for nothing. If I go beyond myself, it is to God. As David said, when he was mocked by Michal for dancing before the ark, “It is to the #smallcaps[Lord]” (2 Sam 6:20, 21). Bonus ludus, a good dance, where Michal scoffs and David dances. Where gracious men magnify God and have Michals to scoff at them, it is bonus ludus. God will look on them, for it is to the Lord. Let us labor that our aims are good, and it does not matter what the world judges of them.
+ And when all this will not do, let us commend our credit to God by prayer. As we commend our souls and conditions, so let us commend our reputations to him, that he would take care of them, that he would bring our righteousness to light, that it may shine out like the noonday. So David does: he complains to God and commends all to him, and prays him to take part against his enemies and to right his cause. And when we have done that, we have done our duty. Yet let us also hope for better things, and be content to pass through the world as unknown men, and to be inwardly worthy, and to pass as unknown men. Rich men, if they are truly rich, will applaud themselves in their own hearts; though the world disgraces them, yet at home, they say, I am furnished in this way. And so it is with a Christian who knows his worth: that he is a child of God, an heir of heaven, that he is attended by angels, that he is a jewel to God in his esteem, absolutely the best thing in the world. He knows the worth of a Christian, and his own worth as a Christian. He applauds and comforts himself in that he knows he has a hidden life, a state of glory hidden in Christ. Now it is covered with disgrace and disrespect in the world, scorned and reproached; but what is that to him? It is a hidden life, and for the present he knows his own excellency, and therefore he can pass through good report and bad report. “I do not care for man’s day,” says Paul; “there is another day before which I must stand” (1 Cor 4:3).
]

And if we do this, as Peter says, “A spirit of glory will rest upon us” (1 Pet 4:14). The ground of comfort we have under rebuke and disgrace is that there is a spirit of glory. What is that? A large spirit, enlarging our hearts with inward comfort, inward joy, inward love of God. “A spirit of glory will rest upon you#super[+],” and will continue with you as long as disgrace continues. He sets this against all the disgrace he meets with in the world.

God at times puts such a glory and excellency on his children under disgrace and ill treatment in the world that he daunts the world by it, as Stephen’s face shone like the face of an angel; this came from a spirit of glory that rested on him, and showed him to be the servant of God. He who takes away from our good name, if we are good, adds to our reward. Our Savior Christ says as much: “Blessed are you#super[+] when you are spoken ill of, for great is your reward” (Matt 5:11, 12).
